{"ID":45,"post_author":"0","post_date":"2026-10-22 23:09:43","post_date_gmt":"2026-10-22 21:09:43","post_content":"<!-- wp:html -->\n<style>\n.thessley-protocol{--bg:#07121f;--panel:#101f32;--panel2:#142740;--ink:#f4f8fc;--muted:#bfd0e2;--cyan:#60d5e8;--blue:#7bb8ff;--violet:#c2a4ff;--gold:#ffd078;--green:#8ce0b2;--line:#35516e;color:var(--ink);background:var(--bg);padding:clamp(1rem,4vw,3rem);border-radius:20px;font-family:inherit;line-height:1.65;color-scheme:dark}\n.thessley-protocol *{box-sizing:border-box}\n.thessley-protocol h1,.thessley-protocol h2,.thessley-protocol h3{color:var(--ink);line-height:1.16}\n.thessley-protocol h1{font-size:clamp(2.4rem,7vw,5rem);letter-spacing:-.045em;max-width:12ch;margin:.4rem 0 1rem}\n.thessley-protocol h2{font-size:clamp(1.65rem,4vw,2.5rem);letter-spacing:-.025em;margin:.2rem 0 .8rem}\n.thessley-protocol h3{font-size:1.2rem;margin:.2rem 0 .6rem}\n.thessley-protocol p,.thessley-protocol li{color:var(--muted)}\n.thessley-protocol a{color:#a9dcff;text-underline-offset:.15em}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-hero{position:relative;overflow:hidden;padding:clamp(1.5rem,5vw,4rem);border:1px solid #284568;border-radius:24px;background:radial-gradient(ellipse at 82% 38%,#1a4569 0,transparent 34%),linear-gradient(130deg,#10243b,#0b1728 65%)}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-kicker,.thessley-protocol .pp-label{color:var(--cyan);font-size:.76rem;font-weight:850;letter-spacing:.13em;text-transform:uppercase}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-hero p{max-width:65ch;font-size:1.1rem}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-chips{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.55rem;margin-top:1.25rem}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-chip{padding:.35rem .7rem;border:1px solid #42617f;border-radius:999px;background:#10243b;color:var(--ink);font-size:.84rem;font-weight:700}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-nav{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:.5rem;margin:1rem 0 2.4rem}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-nav a{padding:.45rem .7rem;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:999px;color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;text-decoration:none}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-nav a:hover{border-color:var(--cyan);color:var(--ink)}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-slide{scroll-margin-top:1.5rem;margin:2.5rem 0;padding:clamp(1rem,3vw,2rem);border:1px solid #263d58;border-radius:20px;background:linear-gradient(145deg,var(--panel),#0c1929)}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-slide-head{display:flex;align-items:flex-start;gap:1rem;margin-bottom:1.2rem}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-num{display:grid;flex:0 0 2.7rem;height:2.7rem;place-items:center;border:1px solid #447191;border-radius:13px;background:#15334a;color:var(--cyan);font-weight:850}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-lead{max-width:72ch;margin:.2rem 0 1.4rem;font-size:1.03rem}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-grid{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,230px),1fr));gap:.85rem}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-card{padding:1rem 1.1rem;border:1px solid #304863;border-radius:15px;background:var(--panel2)}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-card p{margin:.3rem 0 0}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-tag{display:inline-block;margin:0 0 .5rem;padding:.2rem .5rem;border-radius:5px;background:#17445a;color:#a4eff6;font-size:.73rem;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:.05em;text-transform:uppercase}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-figure{overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;padding:.7rem;border:1px solid #2b435e;border-radius:15px;background:#091625}\n.thessley-protocol svg{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;min-width:650px}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-figure figcaption{padding:.5rem .5rem 0;color:#aebfd1;font-size:.88rem}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-code{overflow-wrap:anywhere;padding:1rem;border:1px solid #3a5572;border-radius:12px;background:#081522;color:#d8ecff;font:500 .88rem\/1.8 ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-code strong{color:var(--gold)}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-mark{color:var(--cyan)}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-note{padding:1rem 1.2rem;border-left:4px solid var(--gold);border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;background:#292519;color:#ffebc3}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-note p{margin:.2rem 0;color:#ffebc3}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-step{display:grid;grid-template-columns:auto 1fr;gap:.8rem;align-items:start;margin:.7rem 0}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-step b{display:grid;width:1.8rem;height:1.8rem;place-items:center;border-radius:50%;background:#17445a;color:var(--cyan)}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-step p{margin:0}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-footer{margin-top:2rem;padding-top:1.2rem;border-top:1px solid #29415b;color:#aabdd2}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-arrow{stroke:#60d5e8;stroke-width:3;fill:none;marker-end:url(#ppArrow)}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-arrow-back{stroke:#c2a4ff;stroke-width:3;fill:none;marker-end:url(#ppArrowBack)}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-svg-box{fill:#122944;stroke:#47708f;stroke-width:2}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-svg-phone{fill:#211d3d;stroke:#9b80d7;stroke-width:2}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-svg-target{fill:#19382f;stroke:#67b994;stroke-width:2}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-svg-title{fill:#f4f8fc;font:700 18px system-ui,sans-serif}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-svg-copy{fill:#c3d2e1;font:14px system-ui,sans-serif}\n.thessley-protocol .pp-svg-small{fill:#89dbe8;font:700 12px system-ui,sans-serif;letter-spacing:.04em}\n@media(max-width:600px){.thessley-protocol{padding:.8rem}.thessley-protocol .pp-slide{padding:1rem}.thessley-protocol .pp-hero{padding:1.4rem}.thessley-protocol .pp-nav{margin-bottom:1.4rem}}\n@media(prefers-reduced-motion:no-preference){.thessley-protocol .pp-arrow,.thessley-protocol .pp-arrow-back{stroke-dasharray:9 5;animation:pp-flow 2.4s linear infinite}@keyframes pp-flow{to{stroke-dashoffset:-28}}}\n<\/style>\n<main class=\"thessley-protocol alignwide\">\n  <header class=\"pp-hero\">\n    <p class=\"pp-kicker\">A visual guide \u00b7 Thessley Passkey<\/p>\n    <h1>Your phone signs. The service decides.<\/h1>\n    <p>A Thessley approval is a short-lived, one-time request. Your phone signs the decision with its enrolled private key; the website or computer verifies that signature before continuing the login.<\/p>\n    <div class=\"pp-chips\"><span class=\"pp-chip\">private key stays on device<\/span><span class=\"pp-chip\">fresh request + nonce<\/span><span class=\"pp-chip\">server verifies before access<\/span><\/div>\n  <\/header>\n  <nav class=\"pp-nav\" aria-label=\"Presentation sections\"><a href=\"#overview\">Overview<\/a><a href=\"#enrollment\">Enrollment<\/a><a href=\"#key-storage\">Where the key lives<\/a><a href=\"#approval\">Approval flow<\/a><a href=\"#messages\">Signed messages<\/a><a href=\"#where\">Where it fits<\/a><a href=\"#boundaries\">Security boundaries<\/a><\/nav>\n\n  <section class=\"pp-slide\" id=\"overview\">\n    <div class=\"pp-slide-head\"><span class=\"pp-num\">01<\/span><div><p class=\"pp-label\">The idea<\/p><h2>QR carries a request, never the key<\/h2><\/div><\/div>\n    <p class=\"pp-lead\">The computer or website creates a request and displays a QR code or app link. The phone reads enough information to identify that request, asks you to approve or deny, then returns a signed answer.<\/p>\n    <figure class=\"pp-figure\">\n      <svg viewBox=\"0 0 1000 320\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"pp-overview-title pp-overview-desc\">\n        <title id=\"pp-overview-title\">Thessley approval at a glance<\/title><desc id=\"pp-overview-desc\">A user starts a login. The service creates a request, the phone signs the user's approval, and the service verifies it before allowing access.<\/desc>\n        <defs><marker id=\"ppArrow\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L9,3 z\" fill=\"#60d5e8\"\/><\/marker><marker id=\"ppArrowBack\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L9,3 z\" fill=\"#c2a4ff\"\/><\/marker><\/defs>\n        <rect class=\"pp-svg-box\" x=\"35\" y=\"70\" width=\"230\" height=\"145\" rx=\"20\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"65\" y=\"112\">1 \u00b7 Start<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"65\" y=\"145\">Website or computer<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"65\" y=\"172\">creates a fresh request<\/text>\n        <rect class=\"pp-svg-phone\" x=\"385\" y=\"70\" width=\"230\" height=\"145\" rx=\"20\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"415\" y=\"112\">2 \u00b7 Decide<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"415\" y=\"145\">Phone shows context<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"415\" y=\"172\">and signs your choice<\/text>\n        <rect class=\"pp-svg-target\" x=\"735\" y=\"70\" width=\"230\" height=\"145\" rx=\"20\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"765\" y=\"112\">3 \u00b7 Verify<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"765\" y=\"145\">Service checks key,<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"765\" y=\"172\">signature and request<\/text>\n        <path class=\"pp-arrow\" d=\"M270 125 H370\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"285\" y=\"108\">QR \/ APP LINK<\/text>\n        <path class=\"pp-arrow-back\" d=\"M620 170 H720\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"633\" y=\"200\">SIGNED ANSWER<\/text>\n        <text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"355\" y=\"270\">The service, not the QR image, is the authority that grants or denies access.<\/text>\n      <\/svg>\n      <figcaption>The QR is a convenient handoff. Authorization comes from verifying the phone\u2019s signature against an enrolled public key.<\/figcaption>\n    <\/figure>\n    <div class=\"pp-grid\"><article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">On phone<\/span><h3>Private signing key<\/h3><p>Used to sign an exact message for one request. It is not sent to the computer or website.<\/p><\/article><article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">On service<\/span><h3>Public verification key<\/h3><p>Registered to the account or computer. It lets the service check signatures without learning the private key.<\/p><\/article><article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">In QR\/link<\/span><h3>Request coordinates<\/h3><p>Request ID, nonce, service information and context needed for the app to find and describe the pending request.<\/p><\/article><\/div>\n  <\/section>\n\n  <section class=\"pp-slide\" id=\"enrollment\">\n    <div class=\"pp-slide-head\"><span class=\"pp-num\">02<\/span><div><p class=\"pp-label\">Before first use<\/p><h2>Enrollment connects a phone key to an account<\/h2><\/div><\/div>\n    <p class=\"pp-lead\">The service stores a public key and associates it with the intended user or computer. A matching code or proof-of-possession step helps confirm that the key being enrolled belongs to the phone in front of you.<\/p>\n    <figure class=\"pp-figure\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 1000 360\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"pp-enroll-title pp-enroll-desc\">\n      <title id=\"pp-enroll-title\">Enrollment sequence<\/title><desc id=\"pp-enroll-desc\">A service starts a time-limited enrollment. The phone generates or selects a signing key, returns the public key with proof, and the service verifies and records it.<\/desc>\n      <defs><marker id=\"ppArrow\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L9,3 z\" fill=\"#60d5e8\"\/><\/marker><marker id=\"ppArrowBack\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L9,3 z\" fill=\"#c2a4ff\"\/><\/marker><\/defs>\n      <text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"115\" y=\"38\">Computer \/ website<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"445\" y=\"38\">Thessley app<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"785\" y=\"38\">Enrollment service<\/text>\n      <path d=\"M150 55 V325 M500 55 V325 M835 55 V325\" stroke=\"#425772\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 7\"\/>\n      <path class=\"pp-arrow\" d=\"M835 90 H510\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"605\" y=\"80\">ONE-TIME ENROLLMENT LINK<\/text>\n      <path class=\"pp-arrow-back\" d=\"M500 150 H165\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"235\" y=\"140\">PUBLIC KEY + PROOF<\/text>\n      <path class=\"pp-arrow\" d=\"M160 218 H825\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"395\" y=\"208\">VERIFY KEY, REQUEST AND PAIRING<\/text>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-phone\" x=\"395\" y=\"238\" width=\"210\" height=\"62\" rx=\"14\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"423\" y=\"264\">Private key remains<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"423\" y=\"284\">on the enrolled device<\/text>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-target\" x=\"750\" y=\"238\" width=\"170\" height=\"62\" rx=\"14\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"777\" y=\"274\">Public key saved<\/text>\n    <\/svg><figcaption>Enrollment is its own signed operation; it is not just scanning a QR and trusting whatever key arrives.<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n    <div class=\"pp-note\"><p><strong>Key protection varies by device.<\/strong> The Android app uses Android Keystore. Some devices can protect a key in secure hardware; software-backed keys are also possible. A device\u2019s displayed attestation level is separate evidence and should not be assumed from a successful signature alone.<\/p><\/div>\n  <\/section>\n\n  <section class=\"pp-slide\" id=\"key-storage\">\n    <div class=\"pp-slide-head\"><span class=\"pp-num\">03<\/span><div><p class=\"pp-label\">Key protection<\/p><h2>Where does the private key live?<\/h2><\/div><\/div>\n    <p class=\"pp-lead\">The app asks Android Keystore to create and use the phone\u2019s signing key. Keystore is the API; the device may back that key with software, a TEE, or StrongBox. The service receives the public key and may receive attestation evidence during enrollment\u2014not the private key.<\/p>\n    <figure class=\"pp-figure\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 1000 400\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"pp-storage-title pp-storage-desc\">\n      <title id=\"pp-storage-title\">Android key storage levels and desktop TPM<\/title><desc id=\"pp-storage-desc\">The Thessley app uses the Android Keystore API, which can be backed by software, a Trusted Execution Environment, or StrongBox secure hardware. A separate desktop app may keep a key in a TPM 2.0 chip.<\/desc>\n      <defs><marker id=\"ppArrow\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L9,3 z\" fill=\"#60d5e8\"\/><\/marker><\/defs>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-box\" x=\"335\" y=\"25\" width=\"330\" height=\"72\" rx=\"17\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"394\" y=\"57\">Thessley Passkey app<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"388\" y=\"80\">requests key creation \/ signing<\/text>\n      <path class=\"pp-arrow\" d=\"M500 100 V130\"\/>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-phone\" x=\"335\" y=\"137\" width=\"330\" height=\"64\" rx=\"17\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"415\" y=\"177\">Android Keystore API<\/text>\n      <path d=\"M500 202 V228 M165 228 H835 M165 228 V248 M500 228 V248 M835 228 V248\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#60d5e8\" stroke-width=\"2.5\"\/>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-box\" x=\"35\" y=\"252\" width=\"260\" height=\"112\" rx=\"17\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"65\" y=\"287\">Software-backed<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"65\" y=\"316\">Android software<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"65\" y=\"338\">protects \/ uses key<\/text>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-target\" x=\"370\" y=\"252\" width=\"260\" height=\"112\" rx=\"17\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"400\" y=\"287\">TEE<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"400\" y=\"316\">isolated secure world<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"400\" y=\"338\">inside the processor<\/text>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-phone\" x=\"705\" y=\"252\" width=\"260\" height=\"112\" rx=\"17\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"735\" y=\"287\">StrongBox<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"735\" y=\"316\">separate secure<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"735\" y=\"338\">hardware component<\/text>\n      <text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"352\" y=\"389\">DESKTOP CLIENTS MAY USE A DIFFERENT HARDWARE ROOT: TPM 2.0<\/text>\n    <\/svg><figcaption>TEE means Trusted Execution Environment. StrongBox is a more isolated secure hardware implementation. Availability and actual backing depend on the device and OS.<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n    <div class=\"pp-grid\">\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Android \u00b7 software<\/span><h3>Key operation in software<\/h3><p>The key is managed through Keystore, but the reported security level is software. It does not claim isolation by a TEE or StrongBox.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Android \u00b7 TEE<\/span><h3>Trusted Execution Environment<\/h3><p>A hardware-isolated secure environment, often called the secure world. The key operation is protected from the normal Android environment.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Android \u00b7 StrongBox<\/span><h3>Separate secure hardware<\/h3><p>A dedicated secure component with stronger isolation than the regular TEE path. Only some devices provide it, and it can be slower.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Desktop \u00b7 TPM 2.0<\/span><h3>A different kind of hardware root<\/h3><p>The Thessley desktop client can use a TPM chip. The site\u2019s optional proof checks a manufacturer certificate, activates a fresh attestation key, and verifies that the signing key is fixed to that TPM.<\/p><\/article>\n    <\/div>\n    <div class=\"pp-note\" style=\"margin-top:1rem\"><p><strong>Attestation is evidence about key storage, not the login signature.<\/strong> Normal approval proves that the enrolled key signed this request. Attestation is a separate enrollment-time claim about where that key was generated and protected. TPM means Trusted Platform Module; it is distinct from an Android TEE.<\/p><\/div>\n    <div class=\"pp-grid\" style=\"margin-top:1rem\">\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Android certificate fields<\/span><h3>Two security levels<\/h3><p><code>attestationSecurityLevel<\/code> says where the attestation was produced. <code>keymasterSecurityLevel<\/code> says where the signing key is managed. They are separate fields and can differ.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Current Drupal module<\/span><h3>It does not yet establish key residency<\/h3><p>The module checks the Google certificate chain, enrollment challenge, and match to the registered key, but records only <code>attestationSecurityLevel<\/code>; it does not read <code>keymasterSecurityLevel<\/code>. Its displayed level therefore does not confirm where the signing key lives. Attestation is log-only, enrollment is not blocked, and the verifier has not been validated on real devices.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Linux \/ Windows admin pages<\/span><h3>Parsed, but not trusted<\/h3><p>These pages can display both Android levels, including <code>keymasterSecurityLevel<\/code>, but they do not validate the Android certificate chain against a trusted root. Treat the displayed phone level there as unverified information.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Current TPM reporting<\/span><h3>Verified only after proof<\/h3><p>The optional TPM flow checks the manufacturer chain and key certification. It is limited to supported TPMs and maker roots; a missing or failed proof does not block enrollment. Revocation lists and firmware security are not checked.<\/p><\/article>\n    <\/div>\n    <p class=\"pp-footer\">The desktop hardware module is TPM 2.0 (Trusted Platform Module), separate from Android\u2019s TEE (Trusted Execution Environment).<\/p>\n  <\/section>\n\n  <section class=\"pp-slide\" id=\"approval\">\n    <div class=\"pp-slide-head\"><span class=\"pp-num\">04<\/span><div><p class=\"pp-label\">One approval<\/p><h2>Fresh challenge \u2192 user choice \u2192 verified signature<\/h2><\/div><\/div>\n    <figure class=\"pp-figure\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 1000 430\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"pp-flow-title pp-flow-desc\">\n      <title id=\"pp-flow-title\">Approval request and response sequence<\/title><desc id=\"pp-flow-desc\">The requesting service creates a pending request with a unique identifier and nonce, shows a QR or link, the phone obtains request details and prompts the user, signs approve or deny, and the service verifies and resolves the request.<\/desc>\n      <defs><marker id=\"ppArrow\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L9,3 z\" fill=\"#60d5e8\"\/><\/marker><marker id=\"ppArrowBack\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L9,3 z\" fill=\"#c2a4ff\"\/><\/marker><\/defs>\n      <text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"100\" y=\"38\">Requesting session<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"445\" y=\"38\">Thessley app<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"780\" y=\"38\">Approval service<\/text>\n      <path d=\"M140 55 V390 M500 55 V390 M840 55 V390\" stroke=\"#425772\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 7\"\/>\n      <path class=\"pp-arrow\" d=\"M145 88 H825\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"355\" y=\"78\">CREATE REQUEST \u00b7 ID + RANDOM NONCE \u00b7 EXPIRY<\/text>\n      <path class=\"pp-arrow\" d=\"M155 145 H490\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"230\" y=\"135\">QR \/ LINK<\/text>\n      <path class=\"pp-arrow\" d=\"M505 198 H830\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"600\" y=\"188\">FETCH REQUEST CONTEXT<\/text>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-phone\" x=\"395\" y=\"215\" width=\"210\" height=\"58\" rx=\"14\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"421\" y=\"240\">Show who \/ what<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"421\" y=\"260\">is asking<\/text>\n      <path class=\"pp-arrow-back\" d=\"M495 294 H160\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"250\" y=\"284\">APPROVE OR DENY + SIGNATURE<\/text>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-box\" x=\"70\" y=\"315\" width=\"140\" height=\"54\" rx=\"13\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"93\" y=\"348\">Wait \/ poll<\/text>\n      <path class=\"pp-arrow\" d=\"M840 338 H165\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"434\" y=\"328\">VERIFY PUBLIC KEY \u00b7 SIGNATURE \u00b7 FRESHNESS \u00b7 BINDINGS<\/text>\n      <text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"705\" y=\"387\">Only then continue<\/text>\n    <\/svg><figcaption>Even a valid signature is not enough by itself: the service also checks that this request is still pending and belongs to the right account or machine.<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n    <div class=\"pp-grid\"><article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">1 \u00b7 Request<\/span><h3>Unique and short-lived<\/h3><p>The service creates a request ID and random nonce, records the pending state, and presents a QR or app link.<\/p><\/article><article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">2 \u00b7 Human check<\/span><h3>Review the prompt<\/h3><p>The app shows the service and request context available to that flow. The user chooses approve or deny and unlocks signing.<\/p><\/article><article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">3 \u00b7 Verify<\/span><h3>Server decides<\/h3><p>The service checks the enrolled key, signature, timestamp, nonce, request state, and account or machine binding before access proceeds.<\/p><\/article><\/div>\n  <\/section>\n\n  <section class=\"pp-slide\" id=\"messages\">\n    <div class=\"pp-slide-head\"><span class=\"pp-num\">05<\/span><div><p class=\"pp-label\">What is signed<\/p><h2>Two related protocols, separated by design<\/h2><\/div><\/div>\n    <p class=\"pp-lead\">The phone signs a deterministic, pipe-separated message\u2014not arbitrary JSON. The fixed version prefix keeps a website signature from being accepted as a computer-login signature.<\/p>\n    <div class=\"pp-grid\">\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Website accounts<\/span><h3><code>thessley_passkey_v1<\/code><\/h3><p>It binds the response to the site\u2019s origin, request ID, nonce, type, timestamp, and action.<\/p><div class=\"pp-code\"><span class=\"pp-mark\">thessley_passkey_v1<\/span> | type | request_id | <strong>origin<\/strong> | nonce | timestamp | action<\/div><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Computer approvals<\/span><h3><code>thessley_pam_v1<\/code><\/h3><p>It binds the response to the computer identity and the account being approved.<\/p><div class=\"pp-code\"><span class=\"pp-mark\">thessley_pam_v1<\/span> | type | request_id | <strong>machine_id<\/strong> | <strong>username<\/strong> | nonce | timestamp | action<\/div><\/article>\n    <\/div>\n    <figure class=\"pp-figure\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 1000 230\" role=\"img\" aria-labelledby=\"pp-lock-title pp-lock-desc\">\n      <title id=\"pp-lock-title\">Protocol domain separation<\/title><desc id=\"pp-lock-desc\">A website signature is checked only in the website protocol domain, and a PAM signature is checked only in the computer protocol domain.<\/desc>\n      <defs><marker id=\"ppArrow\" markerWidth=\"10\" markerHeight=\"10\" refX=\"8\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L0,6 L9,3 z\" fill=\"#60d5e8\"\/><\/marker><\/defs>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-box\" x=\"55\" y=\"55\" width=\"335\" height=\"115\" rx=\"18\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"88\" y=\"98\">Website domain<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"88\" y=\"130\">origin-bound approval<\/text>\n      <rect class=\"pp-svg-target\" x=\"610\" y=\"55\" width=\"335\" height=\"115\" rx=\"18\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-title\" x=\"643\" y=\"98\">Computer domain<\/text><text class=\"pp-svg-copy\" x=\"643\" y=\"130\">machine + user bound<\/text>\n      <path class=\"pp-arrow\" d=\"M410 95 H590\"\/><path d=\"M490 75 L530 155 M530 75 L490 155\" stroke=\"#ffd078\" stroke-width=\"6\"\/><text class=\"pp-svg-small\" x=\"437\" y=\"202\">NO CROSS-PROTOCOL REPLAY<\/text>\n    <\/svg><figcaption>The distinct signed prefixes are cryptographic labels. A copied response for one protocol cannot satisfy the other protocol\u2019s verifier.<\/figcaption><\/figure>\n    <div class=\"pp-note\"><p><strong>Signature format:<\/strong> ECDSA over SHA-256 using an EC P-256 public key. The server verifies the signature with the enrolled public key and allows only a small clock skew (60 seconds in the implementation).<\/p><\/div>\n  <\/section>\n\n  <section class=\"pp-slide\" id=\"where\">\n    <div class=\"pp-slide-head\"><span class=\"pp-num\">06<\/span><div><p class=\"pp-label\">Where it runs<\/p><h2>One phone app, different enforcement points<\/h2><\/div><\/div>\n    <div class=\"pp-grid\">\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Website login<\/span><h3>WordPress \/ Drupal<\/h3><p>The browser starts a request and displays the QR. The site polls for a result; only the browser that started the request receives the authenticated session.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Linux<\/span><h3>PAM gate<\/h3><p>PAM pauses the protected action while a concurrent approval listener routes the phone\u2019s signed answer to that request. SSH and sudo integrations can use this protocol family.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Windows<\/span><h3>OpenSSH gate<\/h3><p>Windows OpenSSH first checks the configured password or key. A forced command then waits for Thessley phone approval before starting the shell or command.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">VPN<\/span><h3>Certificate + phone<\/h3><p>OpenVPN checks a per-device client certificate and requests a phone approval. Each connection or reconnect needs a fresh approval in this setup.<\/p><\/article>\n    <\/div>\n    <p class=\"pp-note\"><strong>Same cryptographic idea; different access-control hook.<\/strong> The Windows gate runs after OpenSSH\u2019s first factor, while Linux PAM can sit inside the login decision. The VPN also requires its client certificate.<\/p>\n  <\/section>\n\n  <section class=\"pp-slide\" id=\"boundaries\">\n    <div class=\"pp-slide-head\"><span class=\"pp-num\">07<\/span><div><p class=\"pp-label\">What the protocol guarantees<\/p><h2>Strong checks\u2014and clear limits<\/h2><\/div><\/div>\n    <div class=\"pp-grid\">\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Replay resistance<\/span><h3>Nonce + request state<\/h3><p>A signature is tied to a specific pending request. Used, expired, or already-resolved requests are rejected.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Context binding<\/span><h3>Origin or machine\/account<\/h3><p>Website and computer signatures cover different identifying fields, so approval is scoped to the intended service.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Key lifecycle<\/span><h3>Enrollment and revocation<\/h3><p>Only registered public keys are trusted. Removing a device from the service blocks future approvals from that key.<\/p><\/article>\n      <article class=\"pp-card\"><span class=\"pp-tag\">Device assurance<\/span><h3>Hardware is not implied<\/h3><p>Successful signing proves possession of the key, not by itself that the key is hardware-backed or that the whole phone is uncompromised.<\/p><\/article>\n    <\/div>\n    <div class=\"pp-note\" style=\"margin-top:1rem\"><p><strong>Terminology:<\/strong> Thessley Passkey is an app-level challenge-and-signature approval flow. It is not the browser WebAuthn\/FIDO2 login ceremony. Hardware attestation can add evidence for supported devices, but it is optional and distinct from normal signature verification.<\/p><\/div>\n    <p class=\"pp-footer\">Protocol details summarized from the Thessley Passkey website module and the Thessley PAM Linux\/Windows implementations. Message fields and access hooks vary by integration; check that integration\u2019s own setup guide before enabling it.<\/p>\n  <\/section>\n<\/main>\n<!-- \/wp:html -->\n","post_title":"How Thessley Passkeys Work","post_excerpt":"Visual guide to enrollment, QR requests, signed approvals, and protocol boundaries.","post_status":"publish","comment_status":"closed","ping_status":"closed","post_password":"","post_name":"how-thessley-passkeys-work","to_ping":"","pinged":"","post_modified":"2026-10-22 23:15:52","post_modified_gmt":"2026-10-22 21:15:52","post_content_filtered":"","post_parent":0,"guid":"https:\/\/wp-opsec.eagleeye-intelligence.com\/?page_id=45","menu_order":0,"post_type":"page","post_mime_type":"","comment_count":"0","url":"https:\/\/wp-opsec.eagleeye-intelligence.com\/?page_id=45"}
